import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { FieldHero } from "../../demos/field/hero.rx"
import { FieldValidate } from "../../demos/field/validate.rx"
import { FieldMatch } from "../../demos/field/match.rx"
import { FieldNative } from "../../demos/field/native.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Field" subtitle="Labels a control, describes it, validates it and shows its errors.">
    <Demo source="field/hero"><FieldHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="field/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      A field stands on the native input inside it. <code>FieldLabel</code> is a native{" "}
      <code>{"<label for>"}</code> that names the control, so a press on the label focuses or toggles it. The
      input's own constraints (<code>required</code>, <code>type="email"</code>, <code>pattern</code>,{" "}
      <code>minlength</code>, <code>min</code> and the rest) are what native validation checks, and the error shown
      for them is the browser's own message, in the browser's language. <code>:invalid</code> and{" "}
      <code>:user-invalid</code> on the input are the browser's answer and need nothing from the library.
    </p>
    <p>The library adds:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The wiring: the control's <code>aria-describedby</code> lists the descriptions and, while it shows, the
        error; <code>aria-invalid</code> is set while the field is invalid.
      </li>
      <li>
        A <code>validate</code> function, run after the native constraints pass. Its message is also set on the
        input with <code>setCustomValidity()</code>, so <code>:invalid</code> agrees with the field.
      </li>
      <li>
        When to validate: on submit, on blur or on every change, and whenever the browser checks the input itself
        (its <code>invalid</code> event, which a native submit fires).
      </li>
      <li>
        State the browser has no selector for, written as <code>data-*</code> attributes on every part: touched,
        dirty, filled, valid and invalid.
      </li>
      <li>Disabling the control with the field, or with a <a href="../fieldset/">Fieldset</a> around it.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        There is no Field Item part: put each checkbox or radio in its own <code>{"<label>"}</code>, and a{" "}
        <a href="../checkbox-group/">CheckboxGroup</a> or <a href="../radio/">RadioGroup</a> in the field. Validity's
        state is a context, read with <code>use fieldValidity</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="when">When a field validates</h3>
    <p>
      <code>validationMode</code> says when; a <a href="../form/">Form</a> sets it for all its fields, and a field's
      own prop wins. Inside a Form the default is <code>"onSubmit"</code>; outside one it is{" "}
      <code>"onBlur"</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>"onSubmit"</code>: the Form validates every field when it is submitted, and from then on each field
        validates on every change.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>"onBlur"</code>: when the control loses focus. Editing it afterwards clears the error (unless a
        required control is now empty), and the next blur validates again. The demo above works this way, as any
        field outside a Form does unless told otherwise.
      </li>
      <li><code>"onChange"</code>: on every change of the value.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A missing value doesn't count until the user has changed the field or a submit has validated it, so a
      required field isn't marked invalid when someone only tabs through it. A disabled field isn't validated, and
      is neither valid nor invalid.
    </p>

    <h3 id="plain-form">In a plain form</h3>
    <p>
      A field doesn't need a Form. In a plain <code>{"<form>"}</code> the browser checks the constraints when it is
      submitted, blocks the submit and shows its own message bubble on the first invalid control. The field
      listens for that check (the input's <code>invalid</code> event) and validates too, so its{" "}
      <code>FieldError</code> shows the message beside the control. A Form carries <code>novalidate</code>, so
      there the browser never checks and the event never fires.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <code>validate</code> message is installed on the input with <code>setCustomValidity()</code>, so the
      browser counts it as well: once the field has validated and failed, on blur or with <kbd>Enter</kbd> in the
      field, a native submit is blocked until the value changes. <kbd>Enter</kbd> validates first because a submit
      from the keyboard doesn't blur the field. A press on the submit button right after typing is blocked too: in
      the library's tests, in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, the press blurs the field before the browser checks
      it. Try “studio” below.
    </p>
    <Demo source="field/native"><FieldNative /></Demo>

    <h3 id="custom-validation">Custom validation</h3>
    <p>
      <code>validate</code> receives the value, and the values of the named fields of the form it is in. It returns a
      message, or a list of messages, when the value is invalid, and <code>null</code>, <code>""</code> or nothing
      when it is valid. It runs only once the native constraints pass: in the demo below, the length is checked by
      the browser's <code>minlength</code> and the name by <code>validate</code>.
    </p>
    <Demo source="field/validate"><FieldValidate /></Demo>

    <h3 id="errors">Error messages</h3>
    <p>
      <code>FieldError</code> is rendered only while the field is invalid. Without children it shows the error: the
      browser's message for a native constraint, or what <code>validate</code> returned. Several messages are shown
      as a list. Give it children to show your own text instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>match</code> shows an error for one failure only. Its values are the keys of the DOM's{" "}
      <code>ValidityState</code>: <code>"valueMissing"</code>, <code>"typeMismatch"</code>,{" "}
      <code>"patternMismatch"</code>, <code>"tooShort"</code>, <code>"tooLong"</code>,{" "}
      <code>"rangeUnderflow"</code>, <code>"rangeOverflow"</code>, <code>"stepMismatch"</code>,{" "}
      <code>"badInput"</code>, and <code>"customError"</code> for <code>validate</code>. <code>match</code> set to
      true shows it always.
    </p>
    <Demo source="field/match"><FieldMatch /></Demo>

    <h3 id="styling">Styling the state</h3>
    <p>
      Every part of a field carries the field's state: <code>data-invalid</code> and <code>data-valid</code> once
      it has been validated, <code>data-touched</code> once the control has been focused and left,{" "}
      <code>data-dirty</code> while the value differs from the first one, <code>data-filled</code> while it has a
      value, and <code>data-disabled</code> and <code>data-focused</code>. On the native input itself the last two
      are left out: style it with <code>:disabled</code> and <code>:focus</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Prefer <code>[data-invalid]</code> to <code>:user-invalid</code> for a field's control. It follows the
      field's validation mode, and it also counts <code>validate</code>, the <code>invalid</code> prop and a
      Form's <code>errors</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="controls">Other controls</h3>
    <p>
      A field takes one control. <code>FieldControl</code> and <a href="../input/">Input</a> are the same native input, of any <code>type</code>. <a href="../checkbox/">Checkbox</a> and <a href="../switch/">Switch</a> report their checked state
      as the field's value, a <a href="../radio/">RadioGroup</a> the checked radio's value and a{" "}
      <a href="../checkbox-group/">CheckboxGroup</a> its list. A group is named by <code>FieldLabel</code> through{" "}
      <code>aria-labelledby</code>. The control takes the field's <code>name</code> when it has none of its own.
    </p>

    <h3 id="validity">Reading the validity</h3>
    <p>
      <code>FieldValidity</code> provides the field's validity to its children as the <code>fieldValidity</code>{" "}
      context: the <code>ValidityState</code> flags with <code>valid</code> (null until validated), the messages,
      the value validated, and the initial value.
    </p>
    <Code file="field/validity.rx.txt" />

    <h3 id="reset">Resetting</h3>
    <p>
      A form reset isn't a change. When the browser resets the form, the control returns to its default (an
      uncontrolled one) or keeps its owner's value (a controlled one), and the field takes that value without
      validating it. It is no longer touched or validated, and any error it showed is cleared, including one from a{" "}
      <a href="../form/">Form</a>'s server.
      Select, Combobox, Autocomplete, Slider and Number Field don't take part in a reset yet, and neither does a field around
      one.
    </p>

    <h3 id="external">Errors from elsewhere</h3>
    <p>
      <code>invalid</code> marks the field invalid whatever its value, for a decision made outside it. A{" "}
      <a href="../form/">Form</a>'s <code>errors</code> do the same for the field with that name, until its value
      changes. <code>dirty</code> and <code>touched</code> control those states when something else owns them.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="field" />
  </DocsPage>
}
